  "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-x & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100085/assets/479da73ff2382e584ec7eb02ff2517c7fa21322faf5ecbda2d2ff2d65049ef31.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}y{e}^{x}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x}y{e}^{x}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
